{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head 0e058f1320cf5f53b8055083ee367f9cf399c4a5 on request/preadm-diag. I inspected the full 16-path change against main b803d210def65e6e219d4bc60aa2c6855d5abf97, all published artifact bindings and implementation request #8971. The ten pre-admission steps now have useful diagnoses, and the fixed 503, recording and same-act retry behavior hold in the supplied controls. One confidentiality defect remains.\n\nP1 — Redact the complete credential value, including quoted/escaped values and short bearer credentials.\n\nEvidence: packages/room/src/diag.ts@0e058f1320cf5f53b8055083ee367f9cf399c4a5:47–50. The named-value expression stops at whitespace or a quote inside the value; it does not parse the enclosing quotation or escaped quotation. The bearer expression ignores values shorter than eight characters. Subsequent format/entropy detectors do not repair these recognized credential cases.\n\nI added three synthetic controls in an isolated diagnostic checkout: a quoted password containing three ordinary words; JSON.stringify of a password containing an escaped double quote; and an error containing a four-character Bearer value. Both Node diagnosis() and the real Room DO propose.pinObjects path retain credential text. In the DO controls, every existing assertion for the fixed unavailable response, no recorded act, exactly one diagnosis and successful retry of the same signed act passes before the confidentiality assertion fails. These are executed runtime failures, not speculative patterns or compiler failures.\n\nImpact: new operator logs can retain all or part of a credential from an exception that previously reached the client only as a fixed unavailable message. The shared helper also formats persisted job-token last_error at packages/room/src/jobs.ts:207,279,373. Fixing only the pre-admission call site would leave that second sink exposed. This violates request #8971's no-credential condition and R-WS-4. No live credential, provider operation or real disclosure was involved in this review.\n\nRepair: consume complete credential values for recognized assignments, including quoted spaces and escaped delimiters; handle bearer/basic credential syntax without a minimum-length or entropy exemption. Keep the input/message bounds and never-throw sink. Where a recognized sensitive field is malformed or cut before its closing delimiter, fail closed rather than publish the remaining suffix. Add these three cases to unit and real-DO coverage; also exercise the error name and retained job-error use of the shared helper. Preserve the unchanged client response, recording and retry controls. The existing source/report scope is sufficient; this is a repair under the same implementation request.\n\nArchitecture: the Gitseq architecture page's ordinary source storage (layer 1), application/interpreter responsibilities (layer 5), derived diagnostic information (layer 6) and RPC/HTTPS/MCP adapters (layer 7) are relevant. This delivery changes Artroom's Room application and adapter diagnostics; it changes no Gitseq kernel, verified stream or host-binding contract. Within Artroom it leaves the intended admission, signatures, publication and error contracts intact, while the finding prevents fulfillment of the existing credential boundary. No new protocol guarantee needs adoption to repair it.\n\nSecurity: exception name/message are untrusted text crossing into operator logs and, through the shared job helper, durable error fields. The default console sink, injected sink, bounds, authority checks and fixed client messages were examined. report() contains sink/formatting failures. No new signing authority, credential issuance or live-operation authority is exercised by this request. The unresolved defect is the incomplete credential-value handling above.\n\nSimplification: keep the one shared redactor and one diagnosis format. Retain the short step labels and fixed client-facing messages. Extend the existing helper's recognized-value handling rather than add another per-call-site filter. The README should distinguish its pre-merge gate counts from the exact reviewed head: my fresh Room suites contain 157 Node and 425 workerd tests.\n\nIndependent validation: clean detached checkout /tmp/artroom-checker-preadm-v1 at the exact head; npm ci, root npm run typecheck and root npm test all exit 0. Root output records 2,109 passing tests and one skip. git diff --check against b803d210 exits 0, and tracked files remain clean. In /tmp/artroom-checker-preadm-v1-guards at the same head, the focused original suites pass 19 Node and 22 workerd tests. I reran all 37 supplied mutation targets in that isolated checkout; each produces at least one named runtime-red test. The three additional confidentiality controls fail three Node and three workerd assertions. After restoring all mutations and temporary regression additions, the focused suites again pass 19/22 and tracked files are clean. Logs and reproducible scripts are under /tmp/artroom-checker-preadm-v1-*; no killed run is counted and no live action was taken.\n\nThis verdict authorizes no landing. Keep the complete diagnostic scope and repair the exact-head delivery before requesting review again.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d1c54dc9c36b08acc4c38f2a2306d649b3e48c21","binding":"assigned","head":"0e058f1320cf5f53b8055083ee367f9cf399c4a5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:55565db40cd982a0d7e11fe30c532fb4248132b4\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3bcde3c0d4bd2d2b823c876657bb3484e772ad8a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:55565db40cd982a0d7e11fe30c532fb4248132b4","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}